# Settle line endings once, here, instead of per-copy.
#
# Every text file is stored LF in the repository. What lands in a working
# tree is then decided by the checkout (core.autocrlf), so a Windows
# developer can still have CRLF locally without that ever reaching a commit.
#
# Why this file exists: the stored blobs were already LF (every commit so far
# was made with core.autocrlf=true, which normalises on the way in), but
# nothing in the repository said so. That left the convention dependent on
# each developer's local git config: a clone with core.autocrlf=false, or an
# editor writing LF into a CRLF working tree, silently produces a diff where
# every line of every touched file appears changed. That happened during the
# 2026-09-09 session and briefly hid ~17 real changes behind ~200 files of
# whitespace noise.
#
# `git add --renormalize .` was run when this file was added and staged
# nothing, confirming the existing blobs already match the rule below. This
# file changes no content; it makes the existing convention explicit and
# enforced regardless of local config.

* text=auto

# Source and config: text, normalised. Listed explicitly so intent is
# obvious even though `* text=auto` would already infer it.
*.ts      text
*.tsx     text
*.mjs     text
*.cjs     text
*.js      text
*.jsx     text
*.json    text
*.css     text
*.md      text
*.sql     text
*.prisma  text
*.yml     text
*.yaml    text
*.toml    text
*.txt     text

# PowerShell: keep CRLF in the working tree. Windows PowerShell 5.1 is the
# interpreter here and CRLF is its native convention; see start-infra.ps1's
# own header for the related encoding trap (a UTF-8 file with no BOM is read
# as Windows-1252, turning an em-dash into a string-delimiting curly quote).
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
*.psm1    text eol=crlf
*.psd1    text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even when checked out on Windows, or they fail
# to run under sh/bash (a trailing CR becomes part of the shebang).
*.sh      text eol=lf

# No binary assets are tracked today; these are here so the first one added
# is not silently corrupted by line-ending translation.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.avif    binary
*.svg     text
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.eot     binary
*.mp3     binary
*.mp4     binary
*.ogg     binary
*.webm    binary
